msgid "*float or np.ndarray* -- By default the mean of the valid scores. With ``reduction=\"none\"`` the array of group-wise S/sigma_delta scores with the same shape as obs. Groups with insufficient data or zero observed variance are filled with NaN."
msgstr "*float or np.ndarray* -- By default the mean of the valid scores. With ``reduction=\"none\"`` the array of group-wise S/sigma_delta scores with the same shape as obs. Groups with insufficient data or zero observed variance are filled with NaN."

msgid "hydrometrics.validate\\_inputs"
msgstr "hydrometrics.validate\\_inputs"

msgid "Simulated values. An ensemble has one extra trailing axis, ``obs.shape + (n_members,)``."
msgstr "Simulated values. An ensemble has one extra trailing axis, ``obs.shape + (n_members,)``."

msgid "Axis along which a metric will be computed. It only matters for the ``\"propagate\"`` policy, where it defines which slices become NaN."
msgstr "Axis along which a metric will be computed. It only matters for the ``\"propagate\"`` policy, where it defines which slices become NaN."

msgid "How to handle NaN values. ``\"omit\"`` makes the removal pairwise: wherever ``obs``, ``sim`` or the weight is NaN, NaN is set in all three, so the arrays keep their shape and metrics that count only valid values drop exactly those pairs, also along an axis. ``\"propagate\"`` applies the same mask and leaves it to the caller to turn the affected slices into NaN. ``\"raise\"`` raises ``ValueError`` for any NaN."
msgstr "How to handle NaN values. ``\"omit\"`` makes the removal pairwise: wherever ``obs``, ``sim`` or the weight is NaN, NaN is set in all three, so the arrays keep their shape and metrics that count only valid values drop exactly those pairs, also along an axis. ``\"propagate\"`` applies the same mask and leaves it to the caller to turn the affected slices into NaN. ``\"raise\"`` raises ``ValueError`` for any NaN."

msgid "Sample weights for weighted metrics, same shape as ``obs``, non-negative."
msgstr "Sample weights for weighted metrics, same shape as ``obs``, non-negative."

